Let the AES wrappers fail instead of faulting when corecrypto is absent.

The boot now reaches the network stack and dies there:

    panic(cpu 0 caller 0x...): Kernel data abort
      pc  _aes_encrypt_key128 + 0x24
      lr  _tcp_init + 0x11c
      esr 0x96000005      far 0x0000000000000078

esr 0x96000005 is a data abort, ISS 0x05, a level 1 translation fault -- a
null dereference -- and far names the address: 0x78.

The path is tcp_init() -> tcp_tfo_init(), which generates the TCP Fast Open
key:

    static void
    tcp_tfo_init(void)
    {
            u_char key[TCP_FASTOPEN_KEYLEN];
            read_frandom(key, sizeof(key));
            aes_encrypt_key128(key, &tfo_ctx);
    }

and aes_encrypt_key128() is aes_encrypt_key(), whose first line is

    const struct ccmode_cbc *cbc = g_crypto_funcs->ccaes_cbc_encrypt;

g_crypto_funcs is filled in by register_crypto_functions(), whose only caller
is the corecrypto kext. This kernel has no kexts, so the table is NULL. Count
the pointers in struct crypto_functions ahead of ccaes_cbc_encrypt -- four
digest functions, five ccdigest_info, four hmac functions, two ecb modes --
and the field sits at offset 0x78, which is the faulting address exactly.
This is the same absence that read_random() ran into, in its AES form.

Apple already anticipated half of this. Every GCM wrapper in the file reads
its mode and then checks it:

    const struct ccmode_gcm *gcm = g_crypto_funcs->ccaes_gcm_encrypt;
    if (!gcm) {
            return aes_error;
    }

What they did not anticipate is the table itself being missing, and that read
faults before the check can run. So the reads go through

    #define CRYPTO_MODE(field)  (g_crypto_funcs ? g_crypto_funcs->field : NULL)

which makes a missing table behave exactly like a missing mode. The GCM
wrappers then need no other change; their own checks do the work. The four
CBC wrappers have no such check and are given one, placed before the IV
declaration in aes_encrypt_cbc() and aes_decrypt_cbc() because that
declaration is sized from cbc->block_size.

Four dereferences of g_crypto_funcs are left as they are -- the
ccgcm_init_with_iv_fn and ccgcm_inc_iv_fn calls -- because each one sits
after an "if (!gcm) return aes_error" that now catches the missing table.

tcp_tfo_init() ignores the return value, so TCP Fast Open will carry an
uninitialised key. Nothing can come of that here: using it would go through
aes_encrypt_cbc(), which now returns aes_error as well, and this kernel has
no network interface to begin with.

This is a stopgap of the same kind as prng-without-corecrypto.patch, and it
covers AES only. The rest of libkern/crypto -- SHA, MD5, HMAC, ChaCha20 --
has the identical shape and will fault the same way when something reaches
it. Code signing is the one that matters: iOS validates every binary against
a SHA hash through this table, so a real iOS userland needs corecrypto built
into the kernel rather than guarded around, the way libpthread now is.

